> h(n: int in 1..1000) = sum(i, 1, n, 1/i)
  note: 1/i would divide integers by the index i: i taken as real
> continuous h
sum(i, 1, n, 1/i)
  the sum ≈ ∫ 1/i di from 1 to n, plus Euler-Maclaurin corrections
  ∫ 1/i di = log(abs(i)) + C
  integral: log(abs(real(n)))
  + (g(lo) + g(hi))/2: log(abs(real(n))) + (1/real(n) + 1)/2
  + (g'(hi) - g'(lo))/12 - (g'''(hi) - g'''(lo))/720 (evaluated below)
  measured against the sum (relative error):
      at                       exact          integral    + endpoint  + g', g''' 
      n=1                      1              1           0           0          
      n=200                    5.8780309      0.099       0.013       0.00038    
      n=400                    6.5699297      0.088       0.012       0.00034    
      n=600                    6.9749784      0.083       0.011       0.00032    
      n=800                    7.2624523      0.08        0.011       0.00031    
      n=1000                   7.4854709      0.077       0.01        0.0003     
  the remaining error does not shrink: the corrections at the lower end are not small (it is a constant, like Euler's gamma for 1/i)
> fac(n: int in 1..20) = prod(i, 1, n, i)
> continuous fac
prod(i, 1, n, i)
  log of the product ≈ ∫ log(i) di from 1 to n, plus Euler-Maclaurin corrections
  ∫ log(i) di = i*log(i) - i + C
  integral: exp(real(n)*log(real(n)) - real(n) + 1)
  + (g(lo) + g(hi))/2: exp(real(n)*log(real(n)) + log(real(n))/2 - real(n) + 1)
  + (g'(hi) - g'(lo))/12 - (g'''(hi) - g'''(lo))/720 (evaluated below)
  measured against the product (relative error):
      at                       exact          integral    + endpoint  + g', g''' 
      n=1                      1              0           0           0          
      n=4                      24             0.47        0.062       0.00051    
      n=8                      40320          0.62        0.073       0.00051    
      n=12                     4.790016e+08   0.69        0.077       0.00051    
      n=16                     2.092279e+13   0.73        0.079       0.00051    
      n=20                     2.432902e+18   0.76        0.08        0.00051    
  the remaining error does not shrink: the corrections at the lower end are not small (it is a constant factor, like Stirling's sqrt(2 pi)/e)
> decay(n: int in 0..1000) = {
    x = 1.0
    for i in 1..n { x += 0.01 * (-2 * x) }
    x
  }
> continuous decay
loop(i in 1..n; x = 1 then x + -0.02*x).x
  each iteration steps by h = 0.02 times a rate: Euler's method for
    x' = -x,  x(0) = 1
  with t = 0.02 * (iterations)
  solution (eigenvalues -1):
    x(t) = exp(-t)
    checked: residual 0 on [0, 20]
